@* Exercise 088 - Render Fragment Caching (advanced).
   Goal:   Stop handing a child a brand-new delegate on every render, which defeats
           any change detection the child does on its parameters.
   Drills: caching a RenderFragment that closes over this component, and the
           referential equality that parameter comparison actually uses.
   Passes: dotnet test --filter FullyQualifiedName~Ex088_ *@
<Ex088_RenderFragmentCaching_Panel Label="@Label" Content="@Content" />

@code {
    [Parameter] public string Label { get; set; } = "";

    /// What the fragment renders. An instance member on purpose - see below.
    public string Body { get; } = "panel body";

    // TODO: hand back the SAME RenderFragment instance every time. It renders
    //   <span class="body">…</span>
    // holding Body. Build it once - a null-coalescing assignment into a field is the
    // usual way, since a field initialiser cannot reach an instance member.
    //
    // Why it matters: a RenderFragment is a delegate, and anything comparing this
    // parameter - the panel's gate here, but equally a memo or a @key - compares it
    // by reference. Note WHICH fragments are dangerous, though: a lambda that
    // captures nothing is cached by the compiler into a static field, so an inline
    // one would be the same instance every time and this exercise would have no
    // teeth. This fragment reads Body, so it closes over `this` and a fresh delegate
    // really is allocated on every evaluation - which is the case that bites in real
    // components, because a fragment worth passing down almost always reads
    // something.
    public RenderFragment Content
        => throw new NotImplementedException("TODO: Ex088 - hand back one cached fragment");
}
